00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.984
So what most people
understand under accessibility

00:02:05.727 --> 00:02:09.078
is stuff like ARIA for screen readers,

00:02:09.576 --> 00:02:10.898
keyboard tabbable,

00:02:11.486 --> 00:02:14.825
colors with a minimum
contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1

00:02:15.458 --> 00:02:19.426
and CSS @media
(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce),

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.485
that is to reduce the motion
for people who get sick from it.

00:02:23.168 --> 00:02:25.362
And the most important thing
about all these things,

00:02:25.387 --> 00:02:28.537
are that they are all testable
with tools or manually.

00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:33.872
So you can have a totally
accessible website by those standards,

00:02:33.897 --> 00:02:37.779
but yeah your website may be
very inaccessible to many people.

00:02:39.093 --> 00:02:41.217
There are several types of disabilities.

00:02:41.242 --> 00:02:44.236
You have visible and
non-visible disabilities

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.043
Non-visible disabilities is a
deaf or hard of hearing person,

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:52.967
a person with autism,

00:02:53.527 --> 00:02:55.295
a person with brain damage,

00:02:55.739 --> 00:02:57.962
chronic illness and depression.

00:02:58.649 --> 00:03:01.536
And also under the
numerous chronic illnesses,

00:03:01.561 --> 00:03:03.740
there are several kinds of disabilities.

00:03:03.996 --> 00:03:07.936
Just to pick a few, someone with,
say, MS that makes their hands shake

00:03:07.961 --> 00:03:11.839
which on the other hand makes it very
difficult to use a smartphone or keyboard.

00:03:12.296 --> 00:03:16.042
So you know non-visible disability,
the person may look healthy to you,

00:03:16.074 --> 00:03:18.951
but they may have many disabilities.

00:03:21.192 --> 00:03:24.505
In our lifetime most of us will
have to deal with either long-term

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.920
or short-term illness,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.430
broken bones, high stress
for long periods of times.

00:03:29.455 --> 00:03:33.133
All kinds of illness or injuries that
can make you temporarily disabled

00:03:33.158 --> 00:03:34.803
or disabled for
the rest of your life.

00:03:36.076 --> 00:03:40.171
So most people aren't born disabled,
but become disabled later in life.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.712
I became disabled later in life.

00:03:43.737 --> 00:03:46.598
When I was 25 years old,
I had a brain hemorrhage.

00:03:47.024 --> 00:03:50.546
And when I had that brain hemorrhage,
it disabled my left arm,

00:03:50.571 --> 00:03:53.059
so I had to re-learn
how to use my left arm.

00:03:53.474 --> 00:03:56.743
And for the first time at the
very young age of 25 years,

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.393
I experienced how hard
it is to use a keyboard

00:03:59.417 --> 00:04:02.123
when you're just recovering
from a brain hemorrhage.

00:04:03.594 --> 00:04:05.931
A year later when I was 26 years old,

00:04:05.956 --> 00:04:10.480
I used to be hard of hearing, but
then I lost the rest of my hearing.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.729
So one day I was still hard of hearing

00:04:12.754 --> 00:04:14.914
and the next day I woke
up completely deaf.

00:04:15.584 --> 00:04:19.427
So I had to like drastically
adjust my life in one day.

00:04:20.776 --> 00:04:23.411
So I don't consider myself disabled,

00:04:23.436 --> 00:04:27.930
rather I am made disabled by the
obstacles created by society.

00:04:30.014 --> 00:04:34.794
A society built only for the
seeing, hearing, talking, walking,

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:37.820
healthy, straight, cis male, white people.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.291
Every time I leave my home, this is what
my life looks like outside when I'm deaf.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.982
I'm constantly
avoiding things and adjusting.

00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:55.993
So now a few quick facts about deafness

00:04:56.018 --> 00:04:58.270
that many people are not aware of.

00:04:58.837 --> 00:05:00.432
There are two kinds of deafness.

00:05:00.457 --> 00:05:02.565
You have deaf with capital D

00:05:03.243 --> 00:05:06.788
which means a person who
culturally identifies as a deaf person.

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:09.809
That those persons are born deaf

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:13.676
or became deaf very early
in their life, as kids

00:05:14.411 --> 00:05:17.417
and their primary language
is sign language.

00:05:18.091 --> 00:05:21.167
Then you have people with lower d deaf,

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:23.121
that's people like me, amongst others

00:05:23.578 --> 00:05:26.019
and we became deaf later in life.

00:05:26.619 --> 00:05:30.079
And like I was hard of
hearing since I was a kid,

00:05:30.104 --> 00:05:31.866
but I had a hearing life

00:05:32.185 --> 00:05:33.922
and later on I became deaf.

00:05:34.563 --> 00:05:38.680
And you have people who have had
perfect hearing their whole lifetime,

00:05:39.091 --> 00:05:40.938
but maybe they had an accident

00:05:40.963 --> 00:05:42.361
or they took some medication

00:05:42.386 --> 00:05:45.965
and it is known that some heavy
medication may destroy your hearing.

00:05:46.468 --> 00:05:50.503
And, from one day to the next, people
went from perfect hearing to deafness.

00:05:52.179 --> 00:05:54.952
And of course their spoken
language is their primary language.

00:05:55.283 --> 00:05:58.550
So they will depend more
on captions and transcripts

00:05:58.575 --> 00:05:59.927
instead of sign language.

00:06:01.737 --> 00:06:06.575
Inclusive design is a design approach
that takes as many individuals,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.158
their background, their needs,
experience and ability into account

00:06:11.183 --> 00:06:12.970
from day one of the project.

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:16.569
Accessibility is a part
of inclusive design.

00:06:16.593 --> 00:06:20.436
We want as many people as possible
to be able to use our products.

00:06:22.003 --> 00:06:24.659
Small examples that have a big impact.

00:06:25.673 --> 00:06:29.224
So this is a screenshot
of deliveroo.com

00:06:30.373 --> 00:06:33.815
I'm from the Netherlands so
we have one of those ordering,

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.438
food ordering is Deliveroo.

00:06:36.605 --> 00:06:38.445
and I have never used Deliveroo.

00:06:38.923 --> 00:06:40.710
I don't know how well
you can see the screen,

00:06:40.735 --> 00:06:43.601
but basically on the screen,
I have already picked my dinner,

00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:45.752
and now I have to pay.

00:06:47.215 --> 00:06:51.454
And on the screen there's nowhere
there is an option, a text field,

00:06:51.824 --> 00:06:53.672
where I can fill in that I'm deaf.

00:06:53.977 --> 00:06:57.027
And imagine something
is wrong with my order

00:06:57.052 --> 00:06:58.740
or the restaurant needs to contact me.

00:06:58.765 --> 00:07:00.984
There's no way that I can
let them know that I'm deaf,

00:07:00.984 --> 00:07:03.533
so they can please send me
an email or a text message

00:07:03.558 --> 00:07:04.752
instead of calling.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.515
And the same goes for the delivery person,

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.370
there is no way I can put
that they should text me.

00:07:11.395 --> 00:07:14.266
Imagine they ring the
doorbell or something is wrong

00:07:14.662 --> 00:07:17.760
they are going to call me, because
I can't let them know I'm deaf.

00:07:18.403 --> 00:07:21.308
So this place lost my
business and my money,

00:07:21.333 --> 00:07:23.238
because it's not accessible to me.

00:07:24.699 --> 00:07:28.517
And I like ordering food, so every
time something goes wrong

00:07:28.541 --> 00:07:29.954
this is the feeling I have.

00:07:32.491 --> 00:07:33.491
No food for me.

00:07:35.328 --> 00:07:39.840
So one of the good things to use
for such a website are chat widgets.

00:07:40.089 --> 00:07:44.501
And, little by little, some
places start using a chat widget,

00:07:44.526 --> 00:07:48.331
but they are not using them optimally
to make them accessible to everyone.

00:07:49.291 --> 00:07:51.477
A lot of them have a bot behind those.

00:07:51.502 --> 00:07:54.190
I, as a deaf person
have no use for a bot.

00:07:55.043 --> 00:07:58.237
So if you're going to implement a chat
widget to communicate with the client,

00:07:58.262 --> 00:08:00.793
you should think about
real people behind it,

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:02.933
reasonable waiting timess

00:08:03.893 --> 00:08:06.445
and the widget should work
with all web browsers.

00:08:06.849 --> 00:08:10.493
In the Netherlands, we have like
a social government, social service

00:08:10.866 --> 00:08:14.011
and they have a chat widget
specially for deaf people,

00:08:14.036 --> 00:08:15.479
because they know we cannot call.

00:08:15.818 --> 00:08:19.440
But the first problem is that you
have to click like three or four times

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:20.700
on the website

00:08:20.724 --> 00:08:22.576
to finally arrive on that chat widget.

00:08:23.448 --> 00:08:27.213
And the chat widget will work with Chrome
or maybe it will work with Firefox

00:08:27.238 --> 00:08:30.133
and every time they develop
or they make upgrades

00:08:30.158 --> 00:08:32.868
it's like a surprise with which
browser is going to work.

00:08:33.478 --> 00:08:36.102
Just make sure it works
in all modern browsers.

00:08:37.154 --> 00:08:40.184
Another thing that makes your
website very inaccessible

00:08:40.209 --> 00:08:41.569
that you may not be aware of is,

00:08:41.594 --> 00:08:43.537
is using a no-reply email.

00:08:44.173 --> 00:08:46.384
So you have your WordPress website

00:08:46.409 --> 00:08:49.862
and you followed all advice to
make it accessible to screen readers,

00:08:49.887 --> 00:08:52.700
the colors are working well,
your links work well,

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.731
but then when you interact with clients,
you will send email with a no-reply

00:08:58.011 --> 00:09:00.259
and there will be no contact info

00:09:00.929 --> 00:09:04.137
and the only thing you'll be
providing is a phone number.

00:09:04.137 --> 00:09:06.754
And this has happened to me several times

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.847
and then I need to contact that company,

00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:12.728
I have no way to do it.

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:18.827
So if you're gonna use a no-reply email,
make sure you do have options in that email

00:09:18.852 --> 00:09:20.476
for people who cannot call

00:09:21.146 --> 00:09:22.239
to contact you.

00:09:22.263 --> 00:09:25.280
Let it be an email address
that you are reachable at,

00:09:25.775 --> 00:09:27.024
a chat widget

00:09:28.148 --> 00:09:29.741
or just a contact form.

00:09:30.967 --> 00:09:33.927
Another thing is when people write weblogs,

00:09:34.971 --> 00:09:37.278
I mean a lot of us start
WordPress as a weblog

00:09:37.303 --> 00:09:39.818
and then we expand it with
WooCommerce and everything,

00:09:40.906 --> 00:09:43.647
but people write so difficult.

00:09:44.418 --> 00:09:47.025
I, as a developer, many times
I'm looking for things,

00:09:47.049 --> 00:09:51.177
how to do things in PHP, how to do
things in Python, how to do things in CSS

00:09:51.177 --> 00:09:54.594
and I'll stumble across a web blog
and it's written so difficult.

00:09:55.721 --> 00:09:57.131
And it's not only weblogs,

00:09:57.554 --> 00:09:59.741
the same government website also

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:03.979
has to contact many people of all
socioeconomic statuses in the country.

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:08.089
And I don't know if you know,

00:10:08.114 --> 00:10:11.073
but if you make a mistake
in your social things,

00:10:11.308 --> 00:10:12.483
you may get a fine,

00:10:13.462 --> 00:10:16.979
but when you get a letter from them or an
email from them, it's written so difficult.

00:10:17.004 --> 00:10:19.277
I'm a college, university graduate

00:10:19.302 --> 00:10:22.871
and even I have difficulties
reading what are they telling me

00:10:22.896 --> 00:10:24.515
because it's difficult.

00:10:24.895 --> 00:10:28.564
Imagine a person with no education,
who works a low-paid job

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:30.087
and they get a letter like that.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:34.046
So no one ever said
when reading a web blog

00:10:34.317 --> 00:10:37.013
or a website, I wish this
was written more difficult.

00:10:37.635 --> 00:10:39.766
So leave the difficult words out

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.811
and try to write like you're
writing for kids in sixth grade.

00:10:44.064 --> 00:10:46.354
A photo says more than a thousand words.

00:10:47.547 --> 00:10:51.546
This screenshot is of Specsavers
and it's a Dutch website

00:10:52.087 --> 00:10:55.520
and after selling glasses,
they also sell hearing aids.

00:10:56.468 --> 00:10:59.063
And if you see the
photos on this home page,

00:10:59.088 --> 00:11:03.196
they assume that only elderly
people use hearing aids.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:10.189
And that is a huge, huge
assumption that is totally off base

00:11:10.787 --> 00:11:12.020
because like I mentioned,

00:11:12.045 --> 00:11:15.700
I became hard of hearing, oh I think
I was born, nobody knows for sure,

00:11:15.725 --> 00:11:18.807
but when I started school they
discovered I was hard of hearing.

00:11:19.955 --> 00:11:22.435
So I think by the time I was 12 years

00:11:22.459 --> 00:11:24.445
was recommended that I had hearing aids.

00:11:25.333 --> 00:11:27.975
So imagine nowadays you have a business

00:11:28.577 --> 00:11:32.112
and you just make an assumption
of who your client base is

00:11:32.137 --> 00:11:34.411
and you design your website based on that.

00:11:35.165 --> 00:11:38.549
If I was 12 years today in 2020,

00:11:38.881 --> 00:11:42.439
I would want to scrape
hearing aids with colors

00:11:42.464 --> 00:11:46.954
and you know, I've seen some photos,
they are very very much more fun looking,

00:11:46.979 --> 00:11:48.168
than when back then.

00:11:48.594 --> 00:11:51.634
And imagine you have parents who have money

00:11:51.659 --> 00:11:55.079
or willing to spend money to
get their kids great hearing aids

00:11:55.104 --> 00:11:57.439
and they arrive on a website
that looks like this.

00:11:57.919 --> 00:12:01.123
And its something they'll
click away, right away

00:12:01.148 --> 00:12:05.331
because this website assumes it's
only old people who wear hearing aids

00:12:05.359 --> 00:12:09.302
and the products will probably not be
to the liking of a 12-year-old girl.

00:12:09.897 --> 00:12:15.428
So when you have a website and
you focus on commercial end clients,

00:12:15.452 --> 00:12:16.602
think very hard

00:12:17.161 --> 00:12:19.885
of who are actually your client base,

00:12:20.473 --> 00:12:22.870
because it can cost you
money in the long run.

00:12:23.959 --> 00:12:25.470
So captions,

00:12:26.167 --> 00:12:29.350
Facebook made a very very deep research.

00:12:29.375 --> 00:12:32.274
I mean, for them it's easy, because
they can track everything.

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:37.133
And Facebook found out that increased
view time of videos on Facebook

00:12:37.157 --> 00:12:39.809
was for 12 percent
when they added subtitles.

00:12:40.457 --> 00:12:43.777
And they have a huge,
millions of people to test on

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:46.308
because we can count
Facebook and Instagram on it.

00:12:46.886 --> 00:12:49.127
And captions have so many advantages

00:12:49.152 --> 00:12:51.569
it's not only for hard of
hearing and deaf people,

00:12:52.017 --> 00:12:56.072
but watching videos in noisy
places or the silent train coupé

00:12:56.097 --> 00:12:59.264
and imagine you're at the library
studying, and you take a break

00:12:59.526 --> 00:13:02.106
and you want to see some video
you're going to need captions.

00:13:02.853 --> 00:13:06.225
It's also very handy for the
search engine optimization,

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:10.784
because Google cannot index
sound, but Google does index text.

00:13:11.687 --> 00:13:16.740
So if you have captions, Google will find
your website and your videos much faster.

00:13:17.449 --> 00:13:20.716
And when spoken language
is not your primary language,

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.659
so if you are English speaking and
maybe you understand a bit of Spanish,

00:13:26.204 --> 00:13:30.388
but it will be handy to have captions,
maybe in English or Spanish,

00:13:30.413 --> 00:13:32.499
but as long as you can follow extra well.

00:13:33.093 --> 00:13:35.002
And also if the speaker have an accent,

00:13:35.027 --> 00:13:38.274
I know I have an accent,
because I speak four languages

00:13:38.514 --> 00:13:40.410
and not one mother tongue.

00:13:40.434 --> 00:13:42.619
So I know I sound very confusing

00:13:43.046 --> 00:13:46.822
and I'm sure the caption will even help
hearing people follow me along.

00:13:47.439 --> 00:13:52.247
I don't know how many of you
are familiar with the SRT subtitle file,

00:13:52.792 --> 00:13:57.140
but for me this SRT file
is the biggest evidence

00:13:57.605 --> 00:14:01.314
that way more people want
caption than who don't,

00:14:02.099 --> 00:14:04.412
because the SRT files have been popular

00:14:04.436 --> 00:14:07.218
since the internet was fast
enough to download media.

00:14:07.714 --> 00:14:11.977
And there are websites out there,
thousands and thousands of captions

00:14:12.001 --> 00:14:14.165
and they are hugely popular.

00:14:14.727 --> 00:14:18.824
So like everyone needs or
want captions on their videos,

00:14:19.399 --> 00:14:24.010
so it shouldn't be a question if you're
gonna put a video on your website, like

00:14:24.034 --> 00:14:28.103
should I put a caption on a video, it's
gonna cost money, it's gonna cost time,

00:14:28.127 --> 00:14:29.579
but the answer is, yes.

00:14:30.103 --> 00:14:33.009
Because everyone, everyone wants caption

00:14:33.034 --> 00:14:36.439
and sometimes whether they know it
or not they'll prefer captions.

00:14:37.201 --> 00:14:39.236
So this is another huge fact,

00:14:40.177 --> 00:14:43.015
if you're gonna caption,
never ever do this.

00:14:43.899 --> 00:14:46.394
Do not caption ball sounds like this.

00:14:46.419 --> 00:14:49.484
I mean it's just offensive
to deaf people, especially

00:14:49.509 --> 00:14:50.864
and hard of hearing people.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:56.993
But when Janice in Friends is laughing,
that you do caption.

00:14:57.446 --> 00:14:59.898
And why?
Because in this context,

00:14:59.923 --> 00:15:04.365
one of Janice's most annoying
characteristics was her annoying laugh.

00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:06.767
And I can imagine, I'm hard of hearing,

00:15:06.792 --> 00:15:09.885
so I saw the whole of Friends
before I became deaf,

00:15:10.536 --> 00:15:12.487
but now Friends is on Netflix

00:15:12.512 --> 00:15:16.222
and a whole new generation is meeting
Friends for the first time.

00:15:16.247 --> 00:15:19.453
And I can imagine some
who never heard Friends

00:15:19.478 --> 00:15:22.600
who would appreciate exactly
knowing why people are annoyed

00:15:22.625 --> 00:15:24.000
when Janice laughs.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.666
So do think why, when
you caption something,

00:15:27.691 --> 00:15:30.830
if you are doing the correct way
and not in the offensive way.

00:15:32.243 --> 00:15:36.921
So everyone likes pointing out to
me that YouTube has auto captioning.

00:15:37.611 --> 00:15:39.762
Auto-captioning is not good enough.

00:15:40.349 --> 00:15:42.822
The first thing auto-captions
suffer from bias,

00:15:43.344 --> 00:15:45.359
because it doesn't recognize accents

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.418
and that's also a little non-inclusive.

00:15:48.443 --> 00:15:51.142
When you have a team of the same people.

00:15:51.958 --> 00:15:55.569
I don't know, I'm going to pick
because most tech people are white

00:15:55.594 --> 00:15:59.140
and high socioeconomic captioning they
are going to be thinking the same,

00:15:59.165 --> 00:16:00.638
and not everybody is going to think

00:16:00.662 --> 00:16:02.844
"oh, there might be people
who speak with an accent."

00:16:03.561 --> 00:16:05.143
It fails with names.

00:16:05.806 --> 00:16:07.954
It depends on clear articulation.

00:16:07.998 --> 00:16:11.325
If someone mumbles,
it won't caption it right,

00:16:11.992 --> 00:16:15.919
and it also depends how slow
someone speaks.

00:16:16.932 --> 00:16:19.994
In practice, I sometimes
recommend people do this:

00:16:20.019 --> 00:16:22.466
as you know, like, even for a day,

00:16:22.491 --> 00:16:24.464
although I would like
some to do it for a week,

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.236
but if you watch YouTube a lot, mute.

00:16:27.766 --> 00:16:28.909
Mute the sound.

00:16:29.404 --> 00:16:32.594
and watch YouTube on mute
for a whole week long,

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:37.504
and only you're going to have to do it
like deaf people do: depend on captions.

00:16:37.529 --> 00:16:39.946
Add the caption, if there are captions.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.518
Are they going to be good?

00:16:41.543 --> 00:16:44.030
And so I dare people who are hearing,

00:16:44.055 --> 00:16:48.358
for a week or maybe just try a
day of watching YouTube on mute

00:16:48.823 --> 00:16:51.588
and you'll find the
horrors of auto captioning.

00:16:52.411 --> 00:16:57.178
So, for me, the best and good examples
are Netflix and TED.

00:16:57.722 --> 00:17:00.160
And Netflix is the best because

00:17:01.066 --> 00:17:04.558
English is not my first language,
but my first language is Papiamento.

00:17:04.602 --> 00:17:06.169
I don't know how many of you know that,

00:17:06.194 --> 00:17:08.141
because I was
born and raised on Aruba,

00:17:08.724 --> 00:17:12.698
so unfortunately I won't find
Papiamento captions anywhere in the world,

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:15.820
so my next go-to is English

00:17:15.845 --> 00:17:18.316
and not Dutch like many would think,
but English.

00:17:18.792 --> 00:17:20.551
So when I watch a movie on Netflix,

00:17:20.576 --> 00:17:23.344
I will always choose
the English captions first.

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:27.027
But even so, Netflix
has many, many options

00:17:27.052 --> 00:17:30.849
and when I'm watching a movie
from Spain, for example,

00:17:31.352 --> 00:17:33.776
I would put—I won't dub the language,

00:17:33.801 --> 00:17:36.472
I will let them speak Spanish,
because I can lip read some,

00:17:36.906 --> 00:17:39.430
but I will also choose
the Spanish captions.

00:17:39.663 --> 00:17:42.245
Because if I know those
people are speaking Spanish,

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.631
I prefer to see Spanish captions

00:17:45.169 --> 00:17:47.967
and on the other hand if
I'm seeing an English movie,

00:17:47.992 --> 00:17:51.407
I know they're speaking English,
I want to see English caption.

00:17:51.799 --> 00:17:56.590
And again this is me,
that is late deaf person,

00:17:57.146 --> 00:18:02.114
so you should not assume that all late
deaf person has the same preference.

00:18:02.548 --> 00:18:06.113
And that's the attitude,
there is no one deaf person.

00:18:06.138 --> 00:18:11.194
Every deaf person—late
born, late deaf, or early deaf,

00:18:11.219 --> 00:18:13.545
they all have their individual preference

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.973
of how they like a caption
or sign language.

00:18:16.361 --> 00:18:18.898
And Netflix also has a very good thing

00:18:18.923 --> 00:18:21.337
and not for deaf but for blind people,

00:18:21.362 --> 00:18:23.355
and you have audio description.

00:18:24.003 --> 00:18:29.885
So imagine someone who is blind is
watching a horror movie, for example,

00:18:30.339 --> 00:18:33.098
and right now the screen is silent,

00:18:33.122 --> 00:18:37.740
but everyone who can see is looking
at a creep creeping outside the window.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:41.800
And the audio caption will
let the blind person know

00:18:41.824 --> 00:18:44.298
that there is a creeping
person creeping outside.

00:18:44.323 --> 00:18:48.522
So, on that point, I think Netflix
was doing very well up until now

00:18:48.546 --> 00:18:49.754
with captioning,

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.549
but there is also one thing
you have to take into account.

00:18:53.975 --> 00:18:56.080
Yes, when I'm watching a movie,

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:58.479
I don't know how many of
you know One Day at a Time?

00:18:58.504 --> 00:19:00.800
It's a movie about American Puerto Ri—

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:02.520
no, a Cuban American family,

00:19:03.085 --> 00:19:05.841
so they speak English most of the time,

00:19:06.097 --> 00:19:08.708
but the grandma,
a lot of times, speaks Spanish.

00:19:09.379 --> 00:19:11.784
And that's one pet peeve
I have with captioning

00:19:12.427 --> 00:19:15.853
and while the English is the
main language being spoken,

00:19:15.878 --> 00:19:18.395
and the moment a
character speaks Spanish

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.150
they will not caption it at all.

00:19:20.496 --> 00:19:22.445
There will be English,
no Spanish captions,

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:25.735
or maybe they'll just put,
“person is speaking Spanish.”

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.023
And that is also inaccessible,

00:19:28.401 --> 00:19:31.831
so you have to pay attention
to small details like that.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:33.655
And TED

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:38.231
that has captions in all sorts of
lang—in all main languages

00:19:38.256 --> 00:19:40.802
and depends how old,
how long the video has been online.

00:19:41.316 --> 00:19:44.883
They also have podcasts, and those
podcasts have also transcription.

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:49.718
So for me Netflix and TED are the go
to places to see videos and talks,

00:19:50.518 --> 00:19:54.879
and all transcriptions, not only
for the deaf and hard of hearing.

00:19:54.904 --> 00:20:01.200
And you know, I love to acquire
knowledge and follow talks,

00:20:01.225 --> 00:20:04.598
but podcasts are a new
point of inaccessibility.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:07.511
But This American Life understands this.

00:20:08.255 --> 00:20:13.365
They had all their archives transcribed
that were years ago

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.310
 and they did it in a few months.

00:20:15.668 --> 00:20:17.630
They transcribed all their podcasts.

00:20:18.129 --> 00:20:20.533
And since then every time
an episode is published,

00:20:20.558 --> 00:20:23.395
within 24 hours,
it will have a transcription.

00:20:24.032 --> 00:20:28.041
And they have done, you can, I have a link
under this slide you can look it up later

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:29.737
and you have other statistics,

00:20:29.761 --> 00:20:33.028
they did a huge research and
since they have been captioning

00:20:33.268 --> 00:20:35.857
their review—their listeners has gone up,

00:20:35.882 --> 00:20:39.504
because Google can index,
caption—transcriptions.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.444
So they have an increase of visitors,

00:20:42.469 --> 00:20:44.909
they have an increase of unique visitors.

00:20:45.321 --> 00:20:47.894
They can see that the
transcriptions are being read

00:20:47.918 --> 00:20:50.896
and they can see they have
more people visiting their podcast.

00:20:51.193 --> 00:20:54.366
And why transcripts matter,
there's a huge list.

00:20:54.952 --> 00:20:57.680
Because podcasts right
now, without transcriptions,

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:00.354
they are inaccessible to deaf people,

00:21:00.378 --> 00:21:01.737
hard of hearing people,

00:21:01.801 --> 00:21:03.815
people with hearing sensitivity,

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.590
people with ADHD,

00:21:05.614 --> 00:21:07.344
people in noisy environmentss,

00:21:07.368 --> 00:21:11.022
people in silent environments
as the train or a library.

00:21:11.603 --> 00:21:14.465
And transcripts are
also good for the search engine,

00:21:14.778 --> 00:21:17.017
people's primary language isn't English,

00:21:17.386 --> 00:21:19.986
and also for fast
referencing or quoting.

00:21:20.011 --> 00:21:22.573
So imagine you have a
podcast and it was a hit

00:21:22.598 --> 00:21:24.372
and you have it on your WordPress website.

00:21:24.397 --> 00:21:26.821
Your WordPress website is super accessible.

00:21:26.846 --> 00:21:29.092
You have a podcast that just went viral,

00:21:29.117 --> 00:21:31.855
but now you have a problem:
you don't have transcripts.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:33.645
So Google won't be indexing it.

00:21:34.168 --> 00:21:37.821
And you're going to lose
out on a huge group of people

00:21:37.846 --> 00:21:39.187
for which it is inaccessible.

00:21:39.851 --> 00:21:41.866
And that fast referencing or quoting:

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:46.721
imagine a journalist came to your website
to write about your podcast

00:21:46.746 --> 00:21:50.609
and the person has, really doesn't have
time to go listen to huge podcast.

00:21:51.017 --> 00:21:53.362
And in this sense,
transcripts are very good,

00:21:53.387 --> 00:21:56.626
because people can scan them really fast
looking for the point

00:21:56.651 --> 00:21:58.690
and they can quote from it

00:21:58.715 --> 00:22:01.725
and they can reference it in an article
or on another website.

00:22:02.114 --> 00:22:04.937
So the advantages of having
transcripts are enormous.

00:22:05.405 --> 00:22:07.237
So how inclusion works.

00:22:07.896 --> 00:22:11.040
and why I'm going to tell you this,
because when you have a project

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:13.463
and maybe even if it's a small project,

00:22:13.488 --> 00:22:16.255
it's always good to have
someone outside our bubble

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:17.516
giving you feedback

00:22:18.011 --> 00:22:20.013
and their point of view on your product.

00:22:20.432 --> 00:22:23.948
It doesn't help you to have a bubble
of the same people who think like you,

00:22:23.973 --> 00:22:26.100
are the same culture, all of you.

00:22:26.125 --> 00:22:29.489
So diversity is having
a seat at the table.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.106
Inclusion is having a voice,

00:22:32.567 --> 00:22:34.810
and belonging is
having that voice heard.

00:22:35.358 --> 00:22:39.495
I'm a person who can be considered
a diversity in the Netherlands,

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:40.986
because I am not Dutch.

00:22:41.427 --> 00:22:43.467
I'm chronically ill and I'm deaf.

00:22:43.948 --> 00:22:47.291
and many times that happens
that people ask me to join them

00:22:47.316 --> 00:22:49.653
and to be, you know, the other voice.

00:22:50.289 --> 00:22:53.415
And that's very nice to be sitting
there and it's nice to be talking,

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:56.073
but sometimes you talk and
people don't listen.

00:22:56.098 --> 00:22:59.834
And so it's very important
when you expand your bubble

00:22:59.859 --> 00:23:03.833
to really be aware: am I really
listening to this person's voice.

00:23:03.858 --> 00:23:06.481
I'm not saying you have to do
everything someone tells you,

00:23:06.505 --> 00:23:08.311
but one should really listen

00:23:08.809 --> 00:23:10.758
and you know comprehend
what they are being told

00:23:10.782 --> 00:23:12.999
about their website, about the product.

00:23:13.024 --> 00:23:15.363
And that's the best way to be inclusive.

00:23:16.396 --> 00:23:18.283
And now I'm going to
give you a perspective,

00:23:18.308 --> 00:23:23.369
because since I've been doing this,
I got that question many many times:

00:23:23.394 --> 00:23:26.361
but it costs money,
it costs money, it costs money.

00:23:26.386 --> 00:23:29.843
And the first thing you have to
think about when you have a budget

00:23:30.282 --> 00:23:32.735
and you're getting ready
for stuff, whatever project.

00:23:33.085 --> 00:23:36.900
So you look: I'm going to use WordPress,
that's free, thank you, that's free.

00:23:36.925 --> 00:23:38.477
The hosting is going to cost money

00:23:38.502 --> 00:23:40.680
and maybe I will have to hire a programmer,

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:44.262
but, in that budget, you should
also think about accessibility.

00:23:44.287 --> 00:23:46.444
That should also be from day one,

00:23:46.468 --> 00:23:50.420
because if you're going to add it later,
it's going to cost even more money.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:52.868
So day one you have your budget,

00:23:52.893 --> 00:23:55.864
accessibility is going to
be part of your budget.

00:23:55.889 --> 00:23:57.575
And this is the perspective:

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.119
abled people will say
disabled people are only what,

00:24:00.144 --> 00:24:02.551
like five percent increase
in our web audience?

00:24:02.576 --> 00:24:04.824
Who cares about making the web accessible.

00:24:04.849 --> 00:24:09.224
And also abled people: our
sign-ups increased by five percent.

00:24:09.249 --> 00:24:10.727
Bonuses for everyone!

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:13.967
So that's really something
you should always think about

00:24:13.992 --> 00:24:15.626
when you're going to start a project.

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:20.312
So thank you for listening and
do you have questions for me?

00:24:21.577 --> 00:24:27.861
<v Allie> Wonderful, thank you so much,
that was amazing. I learned a ton.

00:24:27.886 --> 00:24:29.563
I was tweeting through the whole thing.

00:24:29.587 --> 00:24:32.102
I really appreciate everything
that you just shared.

00:24:32.906 --> 00:24:34.837
I do have the questions pulled up

00:24:34.862 --> 00:24:38.377
so I will make sure that
we go through them

00:24:38.402 --> 00:24:42.458
from first ones to the
most recent. Let me go back.

00:24:42.916 --> 00:24:48.998
Okay, so the first question that we have:

00:24:49.444 --> 00:24:52.548
How many fonts should I
use when designing a website?

00:24:52.548 --> 00:24:55.200
I found some websites
using more than two fonts.

00:24:56.891 --> 00:25:01.862
<v Darice> Okay the font thing,
I think just not only for disabled people,

00:25:01.887 --> 00:25:04.883
I think… like I like gifs

00:25:04.908 --> 00:25:09.003
and I get annoyed if there are too many
gifs on the website I'm trying to read,

00:25:09.028 --> 00:25:10.418
but one or two is okay.

00:25:10.442 --> 00:25:15.620
Okay, I do actually design
long-read web pages for my job

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:17.780
and I stand by three fonts.

00:25:17.805 --> 00:25:20.209
I will use one for the headers,

00:25:20.209 --> 00:25:21.539
one for the body,

00:25:22.713 --> 00:25:24.492
and one for the block quotes,

00:25:25.789 --> 00:25:29.354
and maybe a fourth one, but just
for the small text, the captions,

00:25:29.378 --> 00:25:32.945
because if I caption a photo, I will
always do that in a monotype space,

00:25:33.415 --> 00:25:34.681
but no more than four.

00:25:35.099 --> 00:25:38.540
You should always make sure they're
like in some flow and to just four.

00:25:38.565 --> 00:25:41.369
I know it's very hard, I mean there
are people who are font experts

00:25:41.394 --> 00:25:43.825
and I don't think I'm a font expert,

00:25:44.281 --> 00:25:47.417
but like on Typekit from
Adobe and Google fonts,

00:25:47.448 --> 00:25:49.287
you can always Google
the font combination

00:25:49.311 --> 00:25:51.575
and they will teach you
what combinations work

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.700
So you don't have to
guess and mix and match,

00:25:53.725 --> 00:25:56.299
but I will keep it four fonts max.

00:25:56.993 --> 00:26:00.678
and which of two are the main ones:
one for the header and one for the body.

00:26:01.314 --> 00:26:05.240
<v Allie> Next question: Do you
see enough changes in diversity

00:26:05.265 --> 00:26:07.723
in relation to digital accessibility?

00:26:07.748 --> 00:26:11.395
How can we improve and
what area needs it most?

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:14.447
What is the first thing you
address when you start a project?

00:26:14.447 --> 00:26:17.260
So that's three questions in one.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:21.493
<v Darice> Okay, I mostly advise people.

00:26:21.517 --> 00:26:24.744
And then it's like,
I don't know how it is,

00:26:24.769 --> 00:26:26.554
I know I'm speaking to
a worldwide audience,

00:26:26.579 --> 00:26:28.900
so I'm going to speak from
a Netherlands perspective.

00:26:28.925 --> 00:26:31.089
and the majority of the
population here is white.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:37.835
And often, industry at international
products to do with web apps and stuff,

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.968
I remember back then there was a huge thing

00:26:39.993 --> 00:26:43.532
because it was a group of
young guys in Silicon Valley

00:26:43.557 --> 00:26:45.224
who made an app for women

00:26:45.976 --> 00:26:47.418
and they had it all wrong.

00:26:47.442 --> 00:26:49.588
Why? Because they didn't
have a woman in the team.

00:26:50.491 --> 00:26:52.757
So you'd have to do it on day one

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:57.373
and you're gonna hire people
and freelancers and try not,

00:26:57.398 --> 00:26:59.642
I know often you listen, you hear a lot is,

00:26:59.667 --> 00:27:01.807
“Yeah, but they don't
fit in the work culture.”

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.239
See that's the problem,
the work culture.

00:27:04.264 --> 00:27:06.652
You want people that
don't fit in your work culture

00:27:06.677 --> 00:27:11.492
because your audience and your clients are
not going to be part of your work culture.

00:27:12.291 --> 00:27:15.531
So look for people who are
very different from you.

00:27:15.556 --> 00:27:18.085
I'm not telling just pick someone
because they're a woman,

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:20.031
because they are brown or
because they are disabled,

00:27:20.056 --> 00:27:22.244
you're gonna pick some
because they know the job,

00:27:22.634 --> 00:27:25.794
but you're not gonna pick them
because they are the same as you.

00:27:26.783 --> 00:27:28.584
So that's the first
thing you have to think.

00:27:29.737 --> 00:27:32.376
<v Allie> All righty, that was a fantastic answer.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:35.647
Could not agree more.
The next one we have up:

00:27:35.854 --> 00:27:38.476
YouTube auto captioning is not perfect,

00:27:38.501 --> 00:27:42.458
but can those captions be
used as a reference to edit

00:27:42.483 --> 00:27:43.760
and fix the errors?

00:27:45.169 --> 00:27:46.757
<v Darice> Okay, oh yes.

00:27:46.782 --> 00:27:48.124
That's what I do at work too.

00:27:48.149 --> 00:27:50.283
We have videos online,
we're captioning them

00:27:50.307 --> 00:27:52.539
and yes the advice is you upload on YouTube

00:27:52.564 --> 00:27:54.799
and YouTube will generate the auto-caption

00:27:54.824 --> 00:27:56.312
and it's going to
be full of mistakes.

00:27:56.718 --> 00:27:59.128
And what you can do
is you can edit that file.

00:27:59.153 --> 00:28:02.438
So it does help that you
don't have to start from scratch.

00:28:02.438 --> 00:28:05.729
So you just get that
auto-generated from YouTube,

00:28:05.754 --> 00:28:08.358
that's going to be full of errors
and still will take some work

00:28:08.384 --> 00:28:10.134
but at least you're not
starting from scratch.

00:28:10.158 --> 00:28:12.435
It will have all the
minutes already noted

00:28:12.876 --> 00:28:14.760
and so you just have
to go through that text

00:28:14.785 --> 00:28:16.338
and correct all the mistakes

00:28:16.753 --> 00:28:20.680
and then you have fine,
well-working captions.

00:28:20.942 --> 00:28:24.969
So yes, that's the advice, but never
just generate it and leave it like that.

00:28:24.994 --> 00:28:26.244
You can correct them.

00:28:27.056 --> 00:28:29.503
<v Allie> Absolutely, that's fantastic.

00:28:30.356 --> 00:28:32.159
All right, next question.

00:28:33.656 --> 00:28:37.416
Captioning is possible
in YouTube, Vimeo, etc,

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:41.571
but how can I use it in HTML-type videos?

00:28:41.597 --> 00:28:43.313
So I guess that means

00:28:43.337 --> 00:28:46.191
videos that they've just
embedded directly into the site

00:28:46.216 --> 00:28:47.563
and not from a third party?

00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:49.440
<v Darice> Well that's a good question.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.845
You have to see the experts on that.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.418
I'm sure today there will be
someone speaking about that.

00:28:55.171 --> 00:28:58.317
Because I'm deaf myself, I'm
not able to test these things

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.074
and create the captions.

00:29:01.598 --> 00:29:04.936
But if I'm not correct—HTML
videos are just embed files,

00:29:04.961 --> 00:29:09.325
so you have to like burn the
captions hard-coded in the video.

00:29:10.057 --> 00:29:13.145
Because right now YouTube gives you
the options turning it on or off

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:14.405
and Vimeo too,

00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:17.541
but if you're gonna embed it in HTML,
then you need to make sure

00:29:17.566 --> 00:29:20.244
that you burn those captions in the video.

00:29:21.108 --> 00:29:23.018
So that's what I can think about, yes.

00:29:23.827 --> 00:29:28.715
<v Allie> Awesome, so this is actually
the last question that I'm seeing, so

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:29.812
but we do have some time

00:29:29.837 --> 00:29:33.401
so if anyone has additional questions,
please put them in the chat

00:29:33.426 --> 00:29:36.597
so that I can grab them, and
pose them to the speaker here.

00:29:36.622 --> 00:29:39.739
So the last question
I'm seeing right now is:

00:29:39.764 --> 00:29:43.134
Is it good to use "here"
when providing links

00:29:43.159 --> 00:29:45.340
and if not what is the right method?

00:29:46.173 --> 00:29:48.212
<v Darice> No, it's not right to use "here".

00:29:48.755 --> 00:29:49.906
[Allie laughing]

00:29:49.931 --> 00:29:53.144
<v Darice> So always, like,
explain what the link is

00:29:53.169 --> 00:29:55.462
and that explanation part going to link,

00:29:56.354 --> 00:29:58.398
but here is where? Yes, no.

00:29:58.961 --> 00:30:02.734
So if I'm going to link to this
talk later on on my website

00:30:02.759 --> 00:30:06.459
I'm going to write, yesterday I gave
a talk to the WordPress Accessibility

00:30:06.484 --> 00:30:08.835
You can view my talk on YouTube

00:30:09.274 --> 00:30:11.874
and I will link "you can
view my talk on YouTube".

00:30:12.881 --> 00:30:15.788
I'm not gonna say you can
view it here, and link here, no.

00:30:16.274 --> 00:30:18.628
<v Allie> All right, so we have
one more that just came in.

00:30:18.954 --> 00:30:20.325
And the question is:

00:30:20.349 --> 00:30:25.058
What are your thoughts on YouTube
community captions being discontinued?

00:30:25.705 --> 00:30:28.786
<v Darice> Okay, I think the
YouTube community is very big.

00:30:28.812 --> 00:30:31.304
I don't think there is one
YouTube YouTube community,

00:30:31.328 --> 00:30:34.926
because inside YouTube
you have several sections.

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:37.186
And I think…

00:30:38.136 --> 00:30:43.183
yes, let's say for example since this
pandemic that we have been sitting at home,

00:30:43.208 --> 00:30:47.755
I've taken a liking to see these interviews
with writers who release new books.

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:49.636
So they are not going
on book tours anymore,

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:51.884
so everyone is talking to bookstores

00:30:52.936 --> 00:30:55.984
on YouTube and several
of them are captioned.

00:30:56.397 --> 00:30:58.901
I'm not 100 percent sure if
it's auto caption or not.

00:30:58.926 --> 00:31:00.524
I'm going to assume
they were corrected,

00:31:00.549 --> 00:31:02.124
because they're pretty good captions,

00:31:03.515 --> 00:31:05.479
but there's some I'm missing.

00:31:05.509 --> 00:31:08.352
I also like seeing night talk
shows from the United States.

00:31:08.376 --> 00:31:11.194
You know, all those
Jimmys—I can't tell them apart,

00:31:11.777 --> 00:31:13.600
but I noticed that Conan

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:15.753
never ever has captions.

00:31:15.777 --> 00:31:17.440
So I never ever, yes,

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:21.148
Conan and lately I just stumbled
upon him in the recommendation

00:31:21.173 --> 00:31:22.821
and I saw he had captions.

00:31:23.096 --> 00:31:26.270
But before that Conan
did not have subtitles on YouTube.

00:31:26.813 --> 00:31:28.487
And I think it's Kimmel,

00:31:29.054 --> 00:31:32.060
Kimmel has subtitles, but his
subtitles are always delayed.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:39.096
And so it's it's very hit and
miss in the YouTube sphere,

00:31:39.388 --> 00:31:41.385
but what I am very upset about

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:44.349
is the decision of Google to
stop community captions.

00:31:44.697 --> 00:31:46.350
I don't know if you people have followed,

00:31:46.375 --> 00:31:50.052
but Google is gonna stop,
the possibility for people,

00:31:50.077 --> 00:31:52.347
like you have a channel
and you have a video

00:31:52.372 --> 00:31:54.800
and someone can write the
captions for you and submit it.

00:31:55.312 --> 00:31:56.843
But they are going to stop that.

00:31:57.875 --> 00:32:02.194
So Google is actively working
against accessibility on that point.

00:32:02.837 --> 00:32:07.097
And it was also huge thing with Twitter
because Twitter started voice tweets.

00:32:07.122 --> 00:32:09.394
And I want to ask this worldwide audience,

00:32:09.419 --> 00:32:12.249
to think twice before you
start using voice tweet,

00:32:12.778 --> 00:32:14.642
because they announced voice tweet.

00:32:14.667 --> 00:32:17.297
And then everybody was like,
okay what about all the deaf

00:32:17.321 --> 00:32:18.501
and hard of hearing people?

00:32:19.001 --> 00:32:25.220
And it turns Twitter hadn't even talked
about it with their accessibility team.

00:32:25.999 --> 00:32:28.638
It turned out they didn't
really have an accessibility team.

00:32:29.299 --> 00:32:32.873
So when people got mad they had
to scramble and get that team set up,

00:32:33.976 --> 00:32:38.371
because you cannot take, Twitter is
highly accessible right now, write the text

00:32:38.396 --> 00:32:42.475
and you're always giving tips how to
keep the text accessible for screen readers

00:32:42.498 --> 00:32:44.159
and now you want to add voice tweets,

00:32:44.742 --> 00:32:46.342
that won't be accessible to deaf.

00:32:46.366 --> 00:32:49.325
So now the voice tweets are
going to launch soon this year,

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:52.986
and the captions will follow later.

00:32:53.628 --> 00:32:56.043
So I'm asking right now to
the worldwide audience

00:32:56.068 --> 00:32:57.944
please do not use voice tweets.

00:32:57.968 --> 00:33:00.559
Just keep writing your
tweets in so many letters.

00:33:01.289 --> 00:33:02.365
<v Allie> There you go.

00:33:03.227 --> 00:33:06.234
All right folks, that's all the
questions that I'm seeing for now.

00:33:06.259 --> 00:33:11.476
So we are going to go ahead and wrap up.

00:33:12.272 --> 00:33:16.456
Thank you Darice so much
for all that fantastic information.

00:33:16.481 --> 00:33:18.716
You made so many amazing points

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.760
and it was such a pleasure and
an honor to hear you talk today